{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdJxYiE98EeeokzMbmbc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Connect flow works again in a local end-to-end test against a fake Intervals server. It hasn't been tested against the real intervals.icu. There were three separate bugs, each written up in `ANSWER.md`:\n\n1. **The button failed for everyone with 401.** It was a plain link, and the browser doesn't send the `Authorization` header when it loads a page that way. That header only goes out on `fetch` calls, so the server never knew who was clicking. Now `/api/intervals/auth/init` takes a signed-in POST and returns `{authorize_url}`. The button calls it through the app's `fetch` helper and then sends the browser to that URL (`server.py`, `static/app.js`).\n2. **Intervals rejected the link with `invalid_request`.** The calendar-sync release (2026-10-02) asked for both READ and WRITE on the same area. On Intervals.icu, WRITE already includes READ, and listing an area twice gets the request rejected. The scope is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` (`config.py`).\n3. **Rivera was disconnected after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire and come with no refresh token. The code assumed a one-hour lifetime and then threw away a token that still worked. Now it only schedules an expiry if Intervals sends one. If Intervals answers 401 (the athlete revoked access), the app shows the reconnect prompt.\n\nIn the local test:\n- The old GET link is gone (404).\n- POST with the bearer token returns the authorize URL with the new scope.\n- The callback stores the token with no expiry.\n- `/status` and `/wellness` both return 200.\n\nRivera needs to click Connect once more, because the old token was already thrown away. After that the connection should stay up.\n\nTwo things I noticed but didn't change: the OAuth client secrets are committed in plain text (the dev one in `config.py`, the staging one in `README.md`) and should be rotated. Also, since these tokens never expire, they should be encrypted where they're stored in prod.\n\nA worklore story, \"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks\", describes these same quirks, and my fixes follow it. Per your ground rules, I didn't send worklore a report on applying it. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1513,"cache_read_input_tokens":39704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1513},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45212876-72ce-4317-8f4e-0a0fa267bf3e","uuid":"21f01296-fbcd-44ea-8be4-4132986b4dea","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:26:54.719Z","request_id":"req_011CgdJxYVqAWsqP2EMYHnz2"}
